WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.112
Who is this for?

2
00:00:01.112 --> 00:00:02.101
Four groups.

3
00:00:02.101 --> 00:00:03.487
Developers build it.

4
00:00:03.487 --> 00:00:07.025
Identity engineers run it
and keep the reviewed files.

5
00:00:07.025 --> 00:00:12.413
Application owners approve each rotation
and confirm their workloads still sign in.

6
00:00:12.413 --> 00:00:15.499
And the I S S Os review the evidence.

7
00:00:15.499 --> 00:00:19.890
The permissions are split cleanly:
the reads hold only read permissions,

8
00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:23.475
and anything that writes is
delegated to a signed-in person.

9
00:00:23.475 --> 00:00:26.798
That matters, because an
unattended app with permission to

10
00:00:26.798 --> 00:00:30.325
update every application could
add a credential to all of them.

11
00:00:30.325 --> 00:00:35.350
Microsoft does offer a self-rolling key
option, but it skips the owner's approval.
